- NEVER use em dashes (—) anywhere: not in chat replies, code comments, commit messages, docs, or generated copy. This applies in every project, all the time. Rewrite the sentence, or use a comma, colon, parentheses, or a period instead. The same goes for en dashes (–) in prose; a plain hyphen or "to" for ranges is fine.
- NEVER attempt to commit if a local build fails.
- CloudFlare workers are not to be deployed using wrangler locally nor from GitHub Actions. They should always use the cloudflare workers GitHub integration.
- NEVER attempt to commit if a local build fails. FIX THE BUILD OR ADAPT THE TESTS.
- NEVER try and overwrite a production secret without asking for permission first.
- Always use wrangler.jsonc for new wrangler based projects
- When the user says "release" or "deploy", it means: confirm the local build passes, run `npm version patch|minor|major` (which updates the changelog, commits, and tags), then `git push --follow-tags`. Default to `patch` unless the change is a new feature (`minor`) or breaking (`major`); ask if unclear. Never run `wrangler deploy` — the CF GitHub integration handles the actual deploy on push.
- Close GitHub issues as their fixes reach production, not when the code is written. Put `Fixes #N` in the commit (GitHub auto-closes on push to the default branch, unlike Sentry, see below), and for anything filed separately close it explicitly once the deploy is verified live, with a comment naming the version and commit, what changed, and any live re-test worth doing. Verified means checked against production, not "the push succeeded". Never close on a green build alone. If part of the original issue is still outstanding, split that into its own issue and close the original rather than leaving it open as a partial.
- When triaging Sentry issues via MCP, always fetch breadcrumbs alongside the issue/event. `get_sentry_resource(resourceType='issue', ...)` returns a summarized view that omits breadcrumbs and full stacktrace details. The breadcrumb trail (clicks with selectors, fetch URLs, console messages) is usually decisive for diagnosing whether an error is our code, an extension, Google Translate, or a WebView injection. Call `get_sentry_resource(resourceType='breadcrumbs', resourceId='<ISSUE-ID>')` in parallel with the issue fetch. Never ask the user to paste event JSON when the MCP can fetch it.
- The Sentry MCP is read-only — there is no tool for resolving/ignoring/assigning issues or updating status. To resolve an issue, use the Sentry REST API directly with the token in `~/.sentryclirc`: `PUT https://sentry.io/api/0/issues/<numeric-id>/` with `{"status":"resolved"}`. Sentry backfills `statusDetails.inCommit` itself when a commit references the short ID. Get the numeric id from `GET /api/0/organizations/<org>/shortids/<SHORT-ID>/`. Note `/issues/<id>/activity/` is a 404; the activity feed is embedded in `GET /api/0/organizations/<org>/issues/<id>/`. Also: when listing unresolved issues, pass `projectSlugOrId` — without it `is:unresolved` quietly returns nothing even when issues exist.
- `Fixes <SHORT-ID>` in a commit message does NOT auto-close a Sentry issue on its own. Sentry only evaluates fix markers for commits attached to a release via `sentry-cli releases set-commits`. If a project's release pipeline only runs `sourcemaps upload`, every release has `commitCount=0` and the markers are inert — verify with `GET /api/0/organizations/<org>/releases/`. Beware the false positive: manually resolving in the UI backfills `statusDetails.inCommit`, which looks identical to an auto-close after the fact. The real test is the `activity` array — `set_resolved by <person>` means a human did it, not the integration. Still write the marker (it costs nothing and works once set-commits is wired), but never report an issue as closed without checking `status`.
- Never wrap commands in `caffeinate` on the user's Mac. Sleep has been broken on this machine for years, so caffeinate buys nothing and just adds a layer that swallows signals (Ctrl+C, traps). When editing existing scripts, strip caffeinate rather than preserving it.
